<h1>INCLUSIVITY &amp; ACCESSIBILITY CONSIDERATIONS</h1>

<p>This programme involves public speaking, impromptu delivery, and peer feedback &mdash; activities that carry different levels of psychological and practical challenge depending on individual circumstances. The following guidance ensures the session is stretching but never exclusionary.</p>

<div class="inclusion-box">
<span class="inclusion-label">Neurodivergent Participants (e.g. ADHD, Autism, Dyslexia, Anxiety Disorders)</span>
<p><strong>Impromptu speaking</strong> can be acutely distressing for some neurodivergent individuals. Mitigations:</p>
<ul>
    <li>In pre-session communication, outline what the day involves so there are no surprises. Name specifically that there will be standing, speaking, and impromptu activities</li>
    <li>During the 60-Second Spotlight, offer the option to choose from two topic cards (reducing the &ldquo;ambush&rdquo; factor) or to prepare for 60 seconds before speaking</li>
    <li>Never force participation. Always offer a reduced-pressure alternative: presenting to the facilitator and one peer rather than the full group</li>
    <li>Provide handouts in advance (digitally) so participants can pre-read if they process written information better than live instruction</li>
    <li>Be aware that some participants may stim, avoid eye contact, or need movement breaks &mdash; none of these are signs of disengagement</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="inclusion-box">
<span class="inclusion-label">Participants with Speech, Hearing, or Physical Differences</span>
<ul>
    <li>If a participant has a stammer or speech difference, never finish their sentences, rush them, or draw attention to the difference. Allow them to complete their thought at their own pace</li>
    <li>For participants with hearing differences, ensure the facilitator faces the room when speaking (not the screen), speaks clearly without exaggerating, and checks understanding regularly</li>
    <li>If a participant uses a wheelchair or has limited mobility, ensure the &ldquo;presentation area&rdquo; is accessible. Adapt standing activities &mdash; presence is about energy and connection, not posture alone</li>
    <li>If BSL interpreters are required, confirm booking at least 2 weeks in advance and seat the interpreter in the participant&rsquo;s sightline</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="inclusion-box">
<span class="inclusion-label">Participants for Whom English Is a Second Language</span>
<ul>
    <li>Impromptu speaking in a second language is significantly harder. For the 60-Second Spotlight, consider allowing these participants to pick their topic card 2 minutes early</li>
    <li>Avoid idioms and cultural references that may not translate. If you use one in your script, briefly explain it</li>
    <li>In pair and group activities, be intentional about groupings &mdash; avoid pairing two ESL speakers together if it limits their practice in English delivery</li>
    <li>Frame the Jargon Audit (Module 2B) as particularly relevant for cross-cultural communication &mdash; what is clear to a native English speaker may be opaque to an international colleague</li>
</ul>
</div>

<div class="inclusion-box">
<span class="inclusion-label">Psychological Safety &mdash; General Principles</span>
<ul>
    <li>The ground rule &ldquo;Be willing to be uncomfortable&rdquo; must be balanced with genuine safety. Discomfort from stretching is productive; distress is not. Read the difference</li>
    <li>All feedback in front of the group must be specific, balanced, and constructive. Developmental points are framed as &ldquo;next time, try&hellip;&rdquo; not &ldquo;you did X wrong&rdquo;</li>
    <li>If a participant becomes visibly distressed, pause the exercise privately. Never continue an activity at someone&rsquo;s expense for the sake of the schedule</li>
    <li>If using video recording for playback, obtain explicit individual consent. Make it clear that footage is for same-day learning only and will be deleted immediately after the session</li>
</ul>
</div>


