<h1>APPENDIX C: SLIDE REDESIGN &mdash; &ldquo;BEFORE&rdquo; SLIDES &amp; ANSWER KEY</h1>

<p>The following three &ldquo;before&rdquo; slides are used in the Module 4 live walkthrough and the Slide Redesign Challenge activity. Each deliberately commits multiple Slide Sins. The &ldquo;after&rdquo; version and facilitator talking points are provided below each one.</p>

<h2>Before Slide 1: &ldquo;The Wall of Text&rdquo;</h2>
<table>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1a3c6e; color:white; padding:20pt; font-size:9pt; line-height:1.3;">
<p style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold; color:white;">Team Performance Update &mdash; Q3 2025</p>
<p>In Q3 2025, the team delivered a total of 847 service interactions across all three regions (South, Midlands, and North), which represents a 12% increase compared to Q2 2025 (756 interactions). Customer satisfaction scores averaged 4.2 out of 5, which is above the company target of 4.0 but below the team&rsquo;s internal stretch target of 4.5. The main areas of concern were response time (average 3.2 hours vs target of 2 hours) and first-contact resolution rate (68% vs target of 80%). The team has identified three root causes: (1) understaffing during peak periods due to annual leave clustering, (2) incomplete handover documentation between shifts, and (3) a backlog in the CRM system that causes duplicate entries. A corrective action plan has been drafted and is pending approval from the Head of Operations. Key actions include: implementing a leave-booking protocol to prevent clustering, standardising handover templates, and escalating the CRM issue to IT for resolution by end of Q4. Budget impact is estimated at &pound;8,500 for the CRM fix and nil for the process changes. The team requests approval to proceed with all three actions immediately.</p>
</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Sins committed:</strong> Wall of Text (Sin 1), Data Overload (Sin 2). The headline describes the topic, not the insight. The slide contains approximately 190 words. No visual hierarchy. The audience will read ahead of the presenter and stop listening.</p>

<h3>After Slide 1: Redesigned</h3>
<table>
<tr><td style="background-color:white; padding:20pt; border:2px solid #ccc;">
<p style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold; color:#c00000;">Service Quality Is Slipping &mdash; Three Fixes Will Bring Us Back on Track</p>
<p style="font-size:11pt; margin-top:16pt;">&bull; Response time 60% over target &mdash; leave clustering and shift handovers are the root cause</p>
<p style="font-size:11pt;">&bull; Three actions drafted: leave protocol, handover templates, CRM fix (&pound;8.5k)</p>
<p style="font-size:11pt;">&bull; <strong>Ask:</strong> Approval to proceed immediately</p>
</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>What changed:</strong> Headline states the insight and the resolution. Three bullets cover problem, solution, and ask. Word count: 42. Detail moves to the appendix or verbal delivery. The audience instantly knows what the slide is about and what is being asked of them.</p>

<h2>Before Slide 2: &ldquo;The Data Overload&rdquo;</h2>
<table>
<tr><td style="background-color:white; padding:20pt; border:2px solid #ccc; font-size:9pt;">
<p style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold;">Monthly Sales Data by Region &mdash; January to September 2025</p>
<p style="font-size:8pt;">[Describe to participants: imagine a dense table with 9 columns (Jan&ndash;Sep) and 6 rows (5 regions + total), every cell filled with numbers to two decimal places, tiny font, no colour coding, no highlights, row and column gridlines making it hard to scan, and a secondary chart crammed in below the table showing trend lines for all 5 regions overlapping in similar colours.]</p>
<p style="font-size:8pt; margin-top:8pt;">Total: Jan 234.56 | Feb 245.78 | Mar 267.89 | Apr 289.01 | May 301.23 | Jun 278.45 | Jul 312.67 | Aug 298.34 | Sep 345.12 | YTD: 2,573.05</p>
<p style="font-size:8pt;">North: Jan 67.23 | Feb 71.45 | Mar 78.90 | Apr 82.34 | May 89.01 | Jun 76.54 | Jul 92.34 | Aug 85.67 | Sep 101.23</p>
<p style="font-size:8pt;">[...3 more regional rows of similar density...]</p>
</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Sins committed:</strong> Data Overload (Sin 2), Font Festival (Sin 4). The headline describes the data rather than the insight. Too much granularity for any audience except the analyst who created it. Overlapping trend lines make comparison impossible.</p>

<h3>After Slide 2: Redesigned</h3>
<table>
<tr><td style="background-color:white; padding:20pt; border:2px solid #ccc;">
<p style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold; color:#c00000;">North Region Is Driving Growth &mdash; Up 51% YTD While South Has Stalled</p>
<p style="font-size:11pt; margin-top:16pt;">[Simple bar chart: two bars only &mdash; North (tall, green) and South (short, amber). Clean axis labels. Large font.]</p>
<p style="font-size:11pt; margin-top:8pt;">&bull; Full regional breakdown available in appendix slide 14</p>
</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>What changed:</strong> Headline states the insight (which regions are winning and which are not). Visual reduced to one simple comparison. Full data moved to appendix. The audience knows exactly what to focus on and what to ask about.</p>

<h2>Before Slide 3: &ldquo;The Clip Art Graveyard&rdquo;</h2>
<table>
<tr><td style="background-color:#e8e8e8; padding:20pt; font-size:9pt;">
<p style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold; color:#336699;">Our Values &amp; Ways of Working!!</p>
<p style="font-size:8pt;">[Describe to participants: imagine 6 generic stock images (handshake, lightbulb, jigsaw puzzle pieces, mountain climber, thumbs up, group of diverse people smiling) arranged randomly across the slide, each with a single-word label underneath (Teamwork, Innovation, Excellence, Growth, Positivity, Diversity). Three different fonts used. A decorative border. An animated star graphic in the corner. Two exclamation marks in the title. A Tesla logo that is slightly pixelated because it was copied from a Google image search rather than the brand asset library.]</p>
</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>Sins committed:</strong> Clip Art Graveyard (Sin 3), Font Festival (Sin 4), Transition Theatre (Sin 6). Generic stock images add no meaning. Single-word labels say nothing actionable. The slide could belong to any company in the world.</p>

<h3>After Slide 3: Redesigned</h3>
<table>
<tr><td style="background-color:white; padding:20pt; border:2px solid #ccc;">
<p style="font-size:18pt; font-weight:bold; color:#c00000;">How We Work: Three Non-Negotiables</p>
<p style="font-size:12pt; margin-top:16pt;"><strong>1. Move fast, fix fast.</strong> Speed over perfection &mdash; iterate, don&rsquo;t wait.</p>
<p style="font-size:12pt;"><strong>2. Challenge the brief.</strong> If the direction is wrong, say so &mdash; silence is agreement.</p>
<p style="font-size:12pt;"><strong>3. Own the outcome.</strong> No handoffs without accountability.</p>
</td></tr>
</table>
<p><strong>What changed:</strong> Generic values replaced with specific, actionable behaviours unique to the team. No stock images. One font. Clean layout. The audience walks away knowing what is expected of them, not just what the company aspires to.</p>


