<h1>APPENDIX E: VIDEO EXEMPLAR CUE SHEET &amp; SOURCING GUIDE</h1>

<p>This programme uses two video exemplar moments. Both are critical for multi-modal learning and should be sourced and tested before delivery. Below are recommended options and facilitation cues.</p>

<h2>Video 1: Storytelling in Action (Module 2A &mdash; 2&ndash;3 minutes)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:25%;">Purpose</th><td>Show participants what a well-structured business narrative looks like when delivered live. Demonstrates SCR framework and the &ldquo;So What?&rdquo; test in practice.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Recommended Sources</th><td>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Option A (Best):</strong> A recorded internal Tesla UK presentation that uses strong narrative structure. Seek permission from the presenter. Internal examples have the highest credibility because participants recognise the context.</li>
    <li><strong>Option B:</strong> A TED or TEDx talk excerpt that demonstrates business storytelling. Recommended: Simon Sinek &ldquo;Start With Why&rdquo; (first 3 minutes &mdash; strong SCR structure), or Brene Brown &ldquo;The Power of Vulnerability&rdquo; (opening 2 minutes &mdash; masterclass in hooking an audience).</li>
    <li><strong>Option C:</strong> A publicly available corporate keynote excerpt. Apple product launches and Patagonia sustainability presentations are strong examples of data-to-story translation.</li>
</ul>
</td></tr>
<tr><th>Cue Point</th><td>Play from [START] to [END] &mdash; mark exact timestamps in your facilitator copy. Keep the clip to 2&ndash;3 minutes maximum. Longer clips lose the room.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Facilitation Notes</th><td>Before playing: &ldquo;Watch for the SCR structure &mdash; can you spot the situation, the complication, and the resolution?&rdquo; After playing: facilitate 2-minute discussion pulling out specific observations.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Video 2: Presence Contrast Clip (Module 3A &mdash; 3&ndash;4 minutes)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:25%;">Purpose</th><td>Show the visible, tangible difference between low-presence and high-presence delivery of the same content. Makes the four pillars of presence concrete and observable.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Recommended Sources</th><td>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Option A (Best):</strong> Record two versions yourself before the session. Deliver the same 60-second script twice: once flat, monotone, fidgeting, reading from notes, no pauses; once with vocal variety, pauses, grounded posture, and eye contact. This is the most powerful option because participants see <em>you</em> modelling the growth, and it demonstrates that presence is a choice, not a trait.</li>
    <li><strong>Option B:</strong> Use a publicly available &ldquo;before and after&rdquo; public speaking coaching clip. Search for &ldquo;public speaking transformation&rdquo; or &ldquo;presentation coaching before and after&rdquo; on YouTube. Select a clip that shows a clear difference in vocal variety, body language, and pacing.</li>
    <li><strong>Option C:</strong> Use two contrasting clips of different speakers: one who is flat and monotone, one who demonstrates strong presence. Ensure both are professional contexts (not comedy or entertainment).</li>
</ul>
</td></tr>
<tr><th>Cue Point</th><td>Play Clip A (poor delivery) in full, then Clip B (strong delivery) in full. Total combined time: 3&ndash;4 minutes maximum.</td></tr>
<tr><th>Facilitation Notes</th><td>Before playing: &ldquo;Same content, two deliveries. Notice what specific behaviours change.&rdquo; After playing: ask the room to call out specific differences. Write them on flip chart &mdash; this becomes the reference for the four pillars teaching.</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="tip-box">
<span class="tip-label">Video Preparation Checklist</span>
<ul>
    <li>Source clips at least 1 week before delivery</li>
    <li>Download clips locally to avoid reliance on internet/streaming on the day (have a backup USB)</li>
    <li>Test playback on the facilitator laptop with the room&rsquo;s AV system &mdash; check both video and audio quality</li>
    <li>If using internal Tesla clips, confirm you have permission from the presenter and any relevant stakeholders</li>
    <li>Prepare a contingency: if video fails on the day, the facilitator delivers the contrast themselves &mdash; a live &ldquo;before and after&rdquo; demo using the same 60-second script</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h1>APPENDIX F: FACILITATOR DELIVERY NOTES &amp; CONTINGENCIES</h1>

<h2>Timing Flexibility Guide</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Adjustment</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Fewer than 8 participants</strong></td><td>Every participant can present in the full-room capstone (skip the breakout phase). Add a second round to the Power Opener drill. The Difficult Room simulation can run as a whole group. You may gain 15&ndash;20 minutes &mdash; use for extended coaching.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>More than 16 participants</strong></td><td>Limit 60-Second Spotlight to 10&ndash;12 volunteers. Run the Difficult Room in three parallel groups. Keep breakout capstone groups at 4&ndash;5 but run four groups. Add a co-facilitator if possible.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Running behind schedule</strong></td><td>Most compressible: Slide Redesign debrief (shorten to partner-only, cut group review, save 8 min). Energiser after lunch (cut to 5 min). Teams Camera Drill (reduce to 3 presenters, save 3 min). Do NOT compress the capstone or the facilitation simulation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Running ahead of schedule</strong></td><td>Add a second Power Opener round. Extend the Difficult Room to three rounds. Add an impromptu Q&amp;A round after capstone where the audience deliberately asks difficult questions.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Video fails on the day</strong></td><td>Facilitator delivers the contrast live &mdash; same 60-second script twice, once poorly and once with presence. For the storytelling video, facilitator tells a strong SCR narrative live and asks participants to identify the elements. This can be equally powerful.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>No second device for Teams drill</strong></td><td>Pair participants up. One sits at the facilitator laptop (camera on), the other stands behind them watching the screen. They switch. Reduces throughput but preserves the learning.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Difficult Participant Scenarios</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:25%;">Scenario</th><th style="width:75%;">Recommended Approach</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Refuses to present</strong></td><td>Do not force. Acknowledge privately at break. Offer a reduced-pressure alternative: presenting to the facilitator and one peer. Many will join voluntarily by the afternoon once the room feels safe.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Dominates discussion</strong></td><td>Acknowledge, then redirect: &ldquo;Great point. I want to hear from someone we have not heard from yet.&rdquo; If persistent, speak privately at break: &ldquo;Your experience is clearly strong &mdash; would you help me draw out the quieter members?&rdquo;</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Sceptical (&ldquo;I already know this&rdquo;)</strong></td><td>Validate: &ldquo;Great &mdash; strong foundation. Can you apply the framework to a situation where you did not get the outcome you wanted?&rdquo; Position the day as refinement, not remediation.</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Becomes emotional during feedback</strong></td><td>Handle with empathy and privacy. Pause the exercise if needed. Normalise: &ldquo;Feedback on how we present feels very personal. That reaction shows you care about getting better.&rdquo;</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>Consistently on phone / disengaged</strong></td><td>First, check in privately: &ldquo;Is everything okay? I noticed you&rsquo;ve been on your phone &mdash; is there something urgent I should know about?&rdquo; If it is not urgent, remind them of ground rule 1 (Be Present) warmly but directly.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Key Facilitation Reminders</h2>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Model everything you teach.</strong> Your own delivery is your most powerful teaching tool. If you tell participants to use pauses, use pauses. You are under observation all day.</li>
    <li><strong>Feedback is a gift, not a weapon.</strong> Lead with what worked. Developmental points framed as &ldquo;next time, try&hellip;&rdquo;</li>
    <li><strong>Energy management is your responsibility.</strong> If the room is flat, change the modality. If it is buzzing, let it run. Read and respond.</li>
    <li><strong>Use the Parking Lot actively.</strong> Return to parked items. Honour the commitment.</li>
    <li><strong>Close every module with a bridge.</strong> The programme should feel like a single journey, not six separate workshops.</li>
    <li><strong>Protect the close.</strong> Never sacrifice the final 20 minutes for earlier overruns. The capstone, action plan, and buddy pairing are the highest-ROI moments of the day.</li>
</ul>

<h1>APPENDIX G: MANAGER BRIEFING PACK</h1>

<p><em>Send to each participant&rsquo;s line manager within 48 hours of the session.</em></p>

<h2>What Your Team Member Covered</h2>
<p>Your team member attended Presentation Skills V3, a full-day programme equipping them with practical skills to communicate, present, and facilitate to a professional standard. The programme covered:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Audience Empathy:</strong> Shifting from &ldquo;what I want to say&rdquo; to &ldquo;what they need to hear&rdquo;</li>
    <li><strong>Strategic Storytelling:</strong> Using narrative frameworks (SCR, PREP) to structure compelling messages</li>
    <li><strong>Simplification:</strong> Leading with the headline (Pyramid Principle) and cutting jargon</li>
    <li><strong>Executive Presence:</strong> Vocal variety, strategic pauses, body language, and managing nerves</li>
    <li><strong>Virtual Presence:</strong> Camera techniques, energy calibration, and managing screen-share</li>
    <li><strong>PowerPoint Design:</strong> Creating visuals that support rather than undermine a message</li>
    <li><strong>Facilitation:</strong> Opening/closing with impact, questioning techniques, managing dynamics and time</li>
</ul>

<h2>What &ldquo;Good&rdquo; Looks Like After This Programme</h2>
<ul>
    <li>They prepare for presentations by thinking about the audience first, not the content first</li>
    <li>They lead with the recommendation and support it with reasons, rather than building bottom-up through data</li>
    <li>Their slides have clear headlines, minimal text, and strong visual hierarchy</li>
    <li>They open meetings and presentations with a clear hook and purpose, not &ldquo;so, um, let&rsquo;s get started&rdquo;</li>
    <li>They use pauses deliberately and speak with greater vocal range</li>
    <li>They show up on camera with energy, proper framing, and eye contact with the lens</li>
    <li>They close meetings with a clear summary, specific assigned actions, and a forward-looking statement</li>
</ul>

<h2>Three Things You Can Do to Support Transfer</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:5%;">#</th><th style="width:30%;">Action</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td><strong>Give them a platform</strong></td><td>Within the next 2 weeks, ask them to present a topic or update in your team meeting. Encourage them to use the frameworks from the programme (SCR, PREP, Billboard Test). This gives them a low-stakes opportunity to practise.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td><strong>Give them specific feedback</strong></td><td>After they present, give them one specific piece of positive feedback tied to the programme: &ldquo;I noticed you led with the headline &mdash; that was really clear&rdquo; or &ldquo;Your pause before the recommendation was effective.&rdquo; Specific feedback reinforces the behaviour.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td><strong>Role-model it yourself</strong></td><td>The techniques in this programme apply to everyone, regardless of seniority. If you start leading with the headline, using the Audience Empathy framework, and opening meetings with Hook &rarr; Purpose &rarr; Contract, your team will follow. Culture is set by what leaders do, not what training teaches.</td></tr>
</table>

